{{History of the United States}}

[[File:United States (+overseas), administrative divisions - en - colored (zoom).svg|thumb|upright=1.35|Current territories of the United States after the [[Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands]] were given independence in October 1994]]

The '''history of the United States''' spans tens of thousands of years of [[Native Americans in the United States|Indigenous]] civilization; their descendants include but may not be limited to 575 [[federally recognized tribes]]. The history of the present-day United States began in 1607 with the establishment of [[Jamestown, Virginia|Jamestown]] in modern-day Virginia by settlers who arrived from the [[Kingdom of England]], and the landing of the ''[[Mayflower]]'' by English pilgrims to [[Plymouth, Massachusetts|Plymouth]] in 1620. In the late 15th century, [[European colonization of the Americas|European colonization]] began and largely decimated [[Indigenous peoples of the Americas|Indigenous societies]] through [[Genocide of indigenous peoples|genocides]], [[American Indian Wars|wars]], and [[Native American disease and epidemics|epidemics]]. By the 1760s, the [[Thirteen Colonies]], then part of [[British America]] and by extension the [[Kingdom of Great Britain]], were established. The [[Southern Colonies]] built an agricultural system on [[Slavery in the United States|slave labor]] and [[Atlantic slave trade|enslaving millions from Africa]]. After the British victory over the [[Kingdom of France]] in the [[French and Indian Wars]], [[Parliament of Great Britain|Parliament]] imposed a series of taxes and issued the [[Intolerable Acts]] on the colonies in 1773, which were designed to end self-governance. Tensions between the colonies and British authorities subsequently intensified, leading to the [[American Revolutionary War|Revolutionary War]], which commenced with the [[Battles of Lexington and Concord]] on April 19, 1775. In June 1775, the [[Second Continental Congress]] established the [[Continental Army]] and unanimously selected [[George Washington]] as its commander-in-chief. The following year, on July 2, 1776, the Second Continental Congress voted for the [[Lee Resolution]] which created the new nation, and on July 4 formalized its independence by issuing the [[United States Declaration of Independence]]. On September 3, 1783, in the [[Treaty of Paris (1783)|Treaty of Paris]], the British acknowledged the independence and sovereignty of the Thirteen Colonies, leading to the establishment of the United States. On September 17, 1787, the [[Constitution of the United States|U.S. Constitution]] was signed by a [[Founding Fathers of the United States|majority of delegates]], and was later ratified by the [[Thirteen Colonies]], leading to the first modern U.S. government.

In the [[1788–89 United States presidential election|1788-89 presidential election]], Washington was elected the nation's first president. Along with his Treasury Secretary, [[Alexander Hamilton]], Washington sought to create a relatively stronger central government than that favored by other [[Founding Fathers of the United States|founders]], including [[Thomas Jefferson]] and [[James Madison]]. On March 4, 1789, the new nation [[Constitutional Convention (United States)|debated, adopted, and ratified]] the [[Constitution of the United States|U.S. Constitution]], which is now the oldest and longest-standing written and codified national constitution in the world.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gorman |first=Sean |title=Goodlatte says U.S. has the oldest working national constitution |url=https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2014/sep/22/bob-goodlatte/goodlatte-says-us-has-oldest-working-national-cons/ |access-date=January 20, 2026 |website=@politifact |language=en-US}}</ref> In 1791, a [[United States Bill of Rights|Bill of Rights]] was added to guarantee [[Natural rights and legal rights|inalienable rights]]. In 1803, Jefferson, then serving as the [[Presidency of Thomas Jefferson|nation's third president]], negotiated the [[Louisiana Purchase]], which doubled the size of the country. Encouraged by available, inexpensive land, and the notion of [[manifest destiny]], the country underwent [[westward expansion]] in a project of [[settler colonialism]] marked by a [[Indian Wars|series of conflicts]] with the continent's [[Native Americans in the United States|indigenous inhabitants]]. The most notable advocate of [[manifest destiny]] was President [[James K. Polk]], who annexed [[Republic of Texas|Texas]] in 1845, and declared war on [[Second Federal Republic of Mexico|Mexico]] the next year. An overwhelming U.S. victory in the [[Mexican-American War]] led to the [[Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo]] in 1848, where the U.S. acquired much of the [[American Southwest]] from Mexico. Whether or not slavery should be legal in the expanded territories was an issue of national contention, and led to increasing tensions over [[slavery]].

Following the election of [[Abraham Lincoln]] as the nation's 16th president in the [[1860 United States presidential election|1860 presidential election]], southern states [[Ordinance of Secession|seceded]] and formed the pro-slavery [[Confederate States of America]]. In April 1861, at the [[Battle of Fort Sumter]], Confederates launched the [[American Civil War|Civil War]]. However, the [[Union army|Union]]'s victory at the [[Battle of Gettysburg]], the [[List of battles with most United States military fatalities|deadliest battle in American military history]] with over 50,000 casualties, proved a [[Turning point of the American Civil War|turning point in the war]], leading to the [[Conclusion of the American Civil War|Union's victory]] in 1865, which preserved the nation. On April 15, 1865, Lincoln was [[Assassination of Abraham Lincoln|assassinated]]. The Confederates' defeat led to the [[Thirteenth Amendment to the United States Constitution|abolition of slavery]]. In the subsequent [[Reconstruction era]] from 1865 to 1877, the national government [[Fourteenth Amendment to the United States Constitution|gained explicit duty to protect individual rights]]. In 1877, white southern Democrats regained political power in the South, often using paramilitary [[Voter suppression in the United States|suppression of voting]] and [[Jim Crow laws]] to maintain [[white supremacy]]. During the [[Gilded Age]] from the late 19th century to the early 20th century, the United States emerged as the world's leading industrial power, largely due to entrepreneurship, [[Second Industrial Revolution|industrialization]], and the [[European emigration|arrival of millions of immigrant workers]]. Dissatisfaction with corruption, inefficiency, and traditional politics stimulated the [[Progressive Era|Progressive movement]], leading to reforms, including to the [[Income tax in the United States|federal income tax]], direct election of [[United States Senate|U.S. Senators]], citizenship for many Indigenous people, [[Prohibition in the United States|alcohol prohibition]], and [[Women's suffrage in the United States|women's suffrage]].

Initially neutral during [[World War I]], the United States [[American entry into World War I|declared war on Germany]] in 1917, joining the successful [[Allies of World War I|Allies]]. After the prosperous [[Roaring Twenties]], the [[Wall Street crash of 1929]] marked the onset of a decade-long global [[Great Depression in the United States|Great Depression]]. President [[Franklin D. Roosevelt]] launched [[New Deal]] programs, including [[Unemployment benefits|unemployment relief]] and [[Social Security (United States)|Social Security]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Richardson |first=Gary |title=Stock Market Crash of 1929 |url=https://www.federalreservehistory.org/essays/stock-market-crash-of-1929 |access-date=January 20, 2026 |website=www.federalreservehistory.org |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=President Franklin Delano Roosevelt and the New Deal {{!}} Great Depression and World War II, 1929 to 1945 {{!}} U.S. History Primary Source Timeline {{!}} Classroom Materials at the Library of Congress {{!}} Library of Congress |url=https://www.loc.gov/classroom-materials/united-states-history-primary-source-timeline/great-depression-and-world-war-ii-1929-1945/franklin-delano-roosevelt-and-the-new-deal |access-date=January 20, 2026 |website=Library of Congress, Washington, D.C. 20540 USA}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=September 21, 2021 |title=Social Security Act (1935) |url=https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/social-security-act |access-date=January 20, 2026 |website=National Archives |language=en}}</ref> Following the [[Attack on Pearl Harbor|Japanese attack on Pearl Harbor]] on December 7, 1941, the United States entered [[World War II]], helping defeat the [[Axis powers]]; [[Nazi Germany]] and [[Fascist Italy]] in the [[European theatre of World War II|European theater]] and, in the [[Pacific War]], [[Empire of Japan|Imperial Japan]] after using [[nuclear weapon]]s on [[Atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki|Hiroshima and Nagasaki]] in August 1945. The war led to the [[Occupation of Japan|US led Allied occupation of Japan]] until 1952 and the [[Allied-occupied Germany]]. Following the end of World War II, the [[Cold War]] commenced with the United States and the [[Soviet Union]] emerging as superpower rivals; the two countries largely confronted each other indirectly in the [[arms race]], the [[Space Race]], propaganda campaigns, and [[proxy war]]s, which included the [[Korean War]] and the [[Vietnam War]]. In the 1960s, due largely to the [[civil rights movement]], social reforms enforced African Americans' constitutional rights of voting and freedom of movement. In 1991, the United States [[Coalition of the Gulf War|led a coalition]] and invaded [[Iraq]] during the [[Gulf War]]. Later in the year, the Cold War ended with the [[dissolution of the Soviet Union]], leaving the United States as the world's sole superpower.

In the [[Post–Cold War era|post-Cold War era]], the United States has been drawn into [[List of modern conflicts in the Middle East|conflicts in the Middle East]], especially following the [[September 11 attacks]], with the [[war on terror]]. In the 21st century, the country was negatively impacted by the [[Great Recession in the United States|Great Recession]] of 2007 to 2009 and the [[COVID-19 pandemic in the United States|COVID-19 pandemic]] of 2020 to 2023. Recently, the U.S. [[2020–2021 U.S. troop withdrawal from Afghanistan|withdrew from the war in Afghanistan]], [[United States and the Russian invasion of Ukraine|intervened in the Russian invasion of Ukraine]], and became militarily involved in the [[Middle Eastern crisis (2023–present)|Middle Eastern crisis]], and both the [[2025 United States strikes on Iranian nuclear sites|2025]] and [[2026 Israeli–United States strikes on Iran|2026]] strikes in Iran during the [[Twelve-Day War]] and [[2026 Iran War]]. The U.S. also refocused on space exploration in the 2020s with its [[Artemis program]], aiming to create a permanent moonbase. Economically, the 21st century seen has seen increased [[Wealth inequality in the United States|Wealth inequality]], and the rise of [[Big Tech]] firms and [[private equity]]. Politically, the 21st century has generally been marked by an increase in prevalence of [[Political polarization in the United States|political polarization]] and [[Populism in the United States|populism]], and well as [[Democratic backsliding in the United States|democratic backsliding]] particularly during the [[Presidency of Donald Trump|administrations of Donald Trump]].

